From: Gilly1970 <gilroyscott@hotmail.com>
Date: Fri, 1 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: fix HW GRO queue mask and empty TCP payload guard

The driver already exposes the hardware aggregation engine through
NETIF_F_GRO_HW, but two details from the original migration are still
missing.

Use the hardware enable bits that correspond to the LRO/GRO RX queues, and
reject packets whose TCP data offset is at or beyond the received length so
empty payloads are not passed into the aggregation path.

Signed-off-by: Gilly1970 <gilroyscott@hotmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h
@@ -49,7 +49,7 @@
 
 #define AIROHA_LRO_PAGE_ORDER		order_base_2(SZ_16K / PAGE_SIZE)
 #define AIROHA_MAX_NUM_LRO_QUEUES	8
-#define AIROHA_RXQ_LRO_EN_MASK		GENMASK(7, 0)
+#define AIROHA_RXQ_LRO_EN_MASK		GENMASK(31, 24)
 #define AIROHA_RXQ_LRO_MAX_AGG_COUNT	64
 #define AIROHA_RXQ_LRO_MAX_AGG_TIME	100
 #define AIROHA_RXQ_LRO_MAX_AGE_TIME	2000
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
@@ -968,7 +968,7 @@ static struct sk_buff *airoha_qdma_rx_gro(struct airoha_queue *q,
 		return NULL;
 
 	data_off = th_off + (th->doff << 2);
-	if (len < data_off)
+	if (len <= data_off)
 		return NULL;
 
 	tcp_win = FIELD_GET(QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_TCP_WIN_MASK, msg3);
